# Block31 preregistration amendment: oriented side interface

This amendment is frozen before target-runner creation or execution.

The relay and comparison formulas are translation and proper-cubic covariant.
They use the physically oriented returned-pair data `(L,f)` and no absolute
axis names.  Exchanging the two writer roles sends `(L,f,g,h)` to
`(L+9f,-f,h,g)` and transports the logical token and Block30 route correctly.

The one-sided comparison hub, however, places its temporary conjunction and
output cells on the `L` side of the central collar.  Its role-exchanged image
is the mirrored hub, not the identical gate word on the identical output
register.  Block31 therefore requires the oriented pair interface as supplied
hardware.  It proves logical side-exchange equivariance, not a physically
self-mirrored controller.

A physically side-exchange-invariant controller must run the mirrored hub or
encode the ordered-pair token into an equivalent symmetric output carrier.
That finite symmetrization is part of the next physical validator/dispatch
compiler.  The runner must reject promotion of the one-sided 288-gate word to
already-closed side-exchange hardware.
